         "id": "b_f778x4o9",
         "type": "heading",
         "props": {
          "level": "h2",
          "style": "h2",
          "html": "The cabins, plainly",
          "align": "left"
         }
        },
        {
         "id": "b_f8q8ekg6",
         "type": "text",
         "props": {
          "html": "<p>Every cabin aboard is a twin or a double, which is what the arithmetic in the table above means: nobody sleeps in a bunk over a stranger. The grades are plain. A lower-deck twin is what the from fare buys, snug and nearest the engine&rsquo;s hum. An upper-deck double adds about a fifth of the fare and a bigger window. Nyren adds two step-free cabins beside her lift.</p><p>You book a grade, not a numbered door. Fiona assigns cabins a month out, solo travellers first, and sailing alone takes a double at a modest supplement, never a doubled fare.</p>"
         }
        }
       ]
      },
